Reach the ASIO host from web-audio-api 1.7.0.

The `cpal-asio` feature compiles cpal's ASIO backend in, but `get_host()` special-cases only
PipeWire and JACK before falling through to `cpal::default_host()` — always WASAPI on Windows.
Nothing ever asks for the ASIO host, so `enumerate_devices_sync()` lists WASAPI devices and the
feature has no observable effect.

Applied against a copy of the crate and measured 2026-08-09: with it, all four ASIO drivers on
Mickey's machine enumerate and open (ASIO4ALL v2 at 5.8 ms output latency, versus 12 ms for the
same interface through WASAPI). See native/README.md for the full table.

NOT APPLIED in the repo, on purpose — two problems this diff does not solve:

  1. It makes ASIO the *only* host. Like the JACK arm it copies, the first host that answers wins,
     so every WASAPI device disappears from the list. A tracker needs to offer both and let the
     user pick, which means changing enumeration, not adding a third `#[cfg]` arm.
  2. ASIO4ALL never exits after the context drops — the process stops responding, holds a lock on
     its own binary, and `taskkill` claims there is no such task. Fine in a probe, not in an app
     someone closes. The nio and UMC drivers both shut down cleanly.

Kept here so the finding survives; the scratchpad it was proved in will not.

  cd $(cargo metadata --format-version 1 | jq -r '...')/web-audio-api-1.7.0   # or a vendored copy
  patch -p0 < native/asio-host.patch

--- src/io/cpal.rs
+++ src/io/cpal.rs
@@ -38,11 +38,19 @@
         }
     }

+    #[cfg(all(feature = "cpal-asio", target_os = "windows"))]
+    {
+        if let Some(host) = preferred_host(cpal::HostId::Asio, "ASIO") {
+            return Ok(host);
+        }
+    }
+
     Ok(cpal::default_host())
 }

 #[cfg(any(
     feature = "cpal-jack",
+    all(feature = "cpal-asio", target_os = "windows"),
     all(feature = "cpal-pipewire", target_os = "linux")
 ))]
 fn preferred_host(host_id: cpal::HostId, host_name: &str) -> Option<cpal::Host> {
